// A device written in Goose (docs/syntax.md, Modules): its functions are
// in goosedev.goose, which goosedev.build (build.sh) makes into C with
// `goose` (examples/goose/setup.sh builds it) and compiles with
// goosedev_shim.c, which calls them under ent's names. goosedev.emit
// gives the check of extern fns the same as LLVM IR, so a function
// declared a fn here is read as Goose compiles it.
//
//   import goosedev
//   for it: Item { T.mixed += mix(it.n as u64) }
//   for it: Item { T.squares += square_sum(it.n as i32) }
//   let n = parse(Bytes, Values)
//   let middle = median(Values)
//
// A Goose program is one instance: its data stacks (where a function
// keeps an array that grows) are the program's, not each thread's, so a
// function that uses one is a proc, called on one thread; Goose's own
// rule is the main thread, which is where ent calls procs (and on many
// ranks it would be a partition's, which `ent build --ranks` warns of).
// Declared a fn, the check says what it writes: what gs_stks points to.
import files

// Whole numbers, as `parse` writes them and `median` reads them.
buffer Values { value: i64 } capacity 4096

// A whole number mixed (splitmix64's finish). No data stack: a fn, which
// a `for` over entities may call on all the cores.
extern fn mix(x: u64) -> u64
// The squares below n added up, by way of an array on Goose's data
// stack: a proc, which a `for` over entities calls on the main thread.
extern proc square_sum(n: i32) -> i64
// The whole numbers of comma-separated text into `into`, in its place:
// how many there are; -1 at a byte that is neither digit nor comma (and
// `into` is empty), -2 where there are more than it holds (and it has
// those that fit).
extern proc parse(text: Bytes, into: mut Values) -> i64
// The median of the values (the lower middle one where they are even),
// by a sorted copy on Goose's data stack; 0 where there are none.
extern proc median(values: Values) -> i64
